# Code Patterns for HelixGuard

## API Design

- All responses include context, not just data
- Error messages include "why" and "how to resolve"
- Use consistent response envelope patterns
- Include metadata for debugging (timestamps, request IDs)

## Component Design

- Every view serves a specific persona: Leadership, Stakeholder, or Build Team
- UI must reflect "Appropriate Visibility" principle
- Components should be composable and reusable
- Prefer configuration over code duplication

## Data Model

- Follow healthcare AI governance data structures
- Status logic: Red > Amber > Green (worst child determines parent)
- Aggregate progress = average attainment across children
- Support multi-dimensional governance views

## File Organization

```
frontend/src/
├── components/
│   ├── common/       # Reusable UI primitives
│   ├── helixguard/   # HelixGuard-specific components
│   └── layout/       # Page structure components
├── pages/            # Route-level components
├── hooks/            # Custom React hooks
├── services/         # API and business logic
├── types/            # TypeScript definitions
└── utils/            # Pure utility functions
```

## Naming Conventions

- Components: PascalCase (`OKRDashboard.tsx`)
- Hooks: camelCase with `use` prefix (`useInitiatives.ts`)
- Utils: camelCase (`calculateProgress.ts`)
- Types: PascalCase (`Initiative.ts`)
- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE

## State Management

- Local state for UI-only concerns
- Context for cross-component shared state
- Keep state as close to usage as possible
- Avoid prop drilling beyond 2 levels

## Testing Philosophy

- Test behavior, not implementation
- Focus on user-visible outcomes
- Mock external dependencies
- Integration tests for critical paths

## Performance

- Lazy load routes and heavy components
- Memoize expensive calculations
- Virtualize long lists
- Optimize bundle size (tree shaking, code splitting)
